bodyText:
Louisiana's tenant duties come from the Civil Code and apply whether or not the lease repeats them.

The tenant must:
- pay the rent as agreed, which, unless the lease says otherwise, is due at the beginning of each rental period and payable at the address you provide (La. Civ. Code arts. 2683, 2703);
- use the property as a prudent administrator and only for the purpose for which it was leased (La. Civ. Code art. 2683);
- return it at the end in the same condition as delivered, except normal wear and tear (La. Civ. Code art. 2683);
- tell you without delay when the property is damaged or needs repair, or when a third person disturbs the tenant's possession, or owe you the resulting damages (La. Civ. Code art. 2688);
- repair damage caused by the tenant's fault or that of people on the property with the tenant's consent, and deterioration beyond normal or agreed use (La. Civ. Code arts. 2687, 2692).

The tenant must also put up with a repair that cannot wait until the lease ends, subject to a possible rent reduction or dissolution (La. Civ. Code art. 2693), and must give you a forwarding address when the lease ends (La. R.S. 9:3251(A)). Your remedies include dissolving the lease for nonpayment or other nonperformance (La. Civ. Code arts. 2704, 2719) and, for misuse, an injunction, dissolution and damages (La. Civ. Code art. 2686). Smoke and carbon monoxide detector rules are covered in the library's detector notes. These duties apply to fixed-term and month-to-month leases alike.

Louisiana has no single list of required lease disclosures, no state form that must be the first page of a lease, and no notice telling tenants whether a just-cause or rent-increase law covers the unit (Louisiana has neither).

The disclosures that do apply, each covered in its own library entry, are:
- the federal lead-based paint disclosure and pamphlet for housing built before 1978;
- written disclosure, before and during the lease, of any pending foreclosure against a residential dwelling and of the tenant's right to notice of it, with a signed notice within seven calendar days after you are served with a notice of seizure (La. R.S. 9:3260.1);
- the statewide sex-offender database notice, required in every written residential lease executed by a real estate licensee on or after January 1, 2002 (La. R.S. 37:1469);
- a written notice before you accept an application fee, for property to be used as the tenant's primary residence, unless it is in an owner-occupied building of no more than four units (La. R.S. 9:3258.1);
- clear and conspicuous disclosure of any automatic renewal term and how to cancel (see the automatic renewal guidance);
- if you waive your warranty against defects, clear and unambiguous language brought to the tenant's attention (La. Civ. Code art. 2699).

Option to buy: Louisiana's residential property disclosure law, which covers homes of one to four units, applies to a transfer by lease with an option to purchase, so if your lease gives the tenant an option to buy, you must deliver the seller's property disclosure document no later than when the tenant exercises the option (La. R.S. 9:3196, 9:3197, 9:3198). These rules apply now. A 2026 law (Act 708, effective January 1, 2027) extends them to vacant residential lots; it does not change them for homes of one to four units.
